      "[08/28/26 10:59:46] INFO     Starting MCP server                  server.py:981\n",
      "                             'mcp-server-qdrant' with transport                \n",
      "                             'stdio'                                           \n",
      "1. **Allergy in one picture**\n",
      "\n",
      "Allergy is best understood as a **specific immune misrecognition**: a person becomes sensitized to an allergen, and later exposure can trigger **IgE-mediated activation of mast cells and basophils**. That produces symptoms ranging from sneezing and itchy eyes to hives, wheeze, vomiting, and, in the most serious cases, **anaphylaxis**. Evidence in the allergy knowledge base emphasizes that **sensitization is not the same as clinical allergy**; history and testing must be interpreted together.  \n",
      "**Source:** *Biomarkers for diagnosis and prediction of therapy responses in allergic diseases and asthma* (Allergy), PMID: **32893900**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32893900\n",
      "\n",
      "2. **Early symptoms: what often appears first**\n",
      "\n",
      "The early pattern often depends on the organ involved:\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Food allergy:** hives, angioedema, oral itching, vomiting, abdominal pain, cough, wheeze, and sometimes anaphylaxis.  \n",
      "  **Source:** *Biomarkers for diagnosis and prediction of therapy responses in allergic diseases and asthma* (Allergy), PMID **32893900**, URL above.\n",
      "- **Respiratory allergy:** sneezing, rhinorrhea, ocular itching, tearing, and allergic conjunctivitis/rhinitis often appearing together. Symptom clustering with asthma can help suggest IgE sensitization, but symptoms alone do not prove it.  \n",
      "  **Sources:**  \n",
      "  - *Examining the Correlation between Allergic Conjunctivitis and Allergic Rhinitis* (PubMed), PMID **37971456**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37971456  \n",
      "  - *Combinations of self-reported rhinitis, conjunctivitis, and asthma predicts IgE sensitization in more than 25,000 Danes* (Allergy), PMID **33900050**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33900050\n",
      "\n",
      "3. **When symptoms become an emergency**\n",
      "\n",
      "The knowledge base is very clear here: **anaphylaxis requires urgent recognition and treatment with epinephrine**. Warning signs include:\n",
      "\n",
      "- multi-system involvement,\n",
      "- **respiratory compromise**,\n",
      "- **hypotension/collapse**,\n",
      "- or **rapidly progressive symptoms after likely allergen exposure**.\n",
      "\n",
      "Localized itching or a few hives are different from an emergency, but progression, breathing difficulty, circulation symptoms, or neurologic change shift the situation into urgent care.  \n",
      "**Sources:**  \n",
      "- *7 for 11: Updates in pediatric anaphylaxis* (PubMed), PMID **41862016**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41862016  \n",
      "- *Standardized Grass Pollen Allergenic Extracts* (FDA), URL: https://www.fda.gov/media/191454/download?attachment=\n",
      "\n",
      "4. **How the allergic immune response works**\n",
      "\n",
      "The established mechanism is an **IgE-centered immune response**. After sensitization, allergen-specific IgE can trigger **mast-cell and basophil activation**. In allergic airway disease, the type 2 immune network includes **IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13**, with epithelial-derived **alarmins** such as **TSLP, IL-33, and IL-25** upstream of Th2 and ILC2 responses. These pathways are important because they explain both symptoms and modern targeted therapy.  \n",
      "**Sources:**  \n",
      "- *Biomarkers for diagnosis and prediction of therapy responses in allergic diseases and asthma* (Allergy), PMID **32893900**, DOI **10.1111/all.14582**  \n",
      "- *The Evolution, Immunopathogenesis and Biomarkers of Type 2 Inflammation in Common Allergic Disorders* (PubMed), PMID **40586319**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40586319\n",
      "\n",
      "5. **Why the environment matters**\n",
      "\n",
      "The environment shapes both exposure and immune risk:\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Dust mites:** indoor humidity, temperature, and housing conditions influence mite growth and allergen load.  \n",
      "  **Sources:**  \n",
      "  - *Life cycle and reproduction of house-dust mites: environmental factors influencing mite populations* (PubMed), PMID **10096801**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10096801  \n",
      "  - *Dust mite allergens: mitigation and control* (PubMed), PMID **12165210**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12165210\n",
      "- **Climate and pollution:** temperature, humidity, pollution, and climate change may modify dust mite growth, survival, and allergen production, though the strength of human disease causation varies by region.  \n",
      "  **Source:** *House Dust Mite Allergy Under Changing Environments* (PubMed), PMID **31172715**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31172715\n",
      "- **Broader exposome:** climate, pollution, biodiversity loss, pathogens, allergens, and cleaning chemicals may affect the epithelial barrier and microbiome. This is a plausible framework, not a single proven explanation for every patient.  \n",
      "  **Source:** *Epithelial Barrier Theory: The Role of Exposome, Microbiome, and Barrier Function in Allergic Diseases* (PubMed), PMID **37957791**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37957791\n",
      "\n",
      "6. **Individual prevention and exposure reduction**\n",
      "\n",
      "The evidence base favors **personalized, multi-component environmental control**, especially for dust mite allergy. Single interventions often fail.\n",
      "\n",
      "What the stored evidence supports:\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Humidity control** matters because indoor humidity strongly affects mite populations.\n",
      "- **Bedding encasings, cleaning, and reducing reservoirs** are part of multi-step control.\n",
      "- **HEPA-only air cleaners are not enough by themselves** as a substitute for standard mite avoidance measures.\n",
      "- Prevention should be **individualized**, not universal, because geography, housing, and exposures differ.\n",
      "\n",
      "For respiratory allergy, **tracking symptoms and medication use together with pollen-season timing** can help plan prevention, but should be tailored rather than assumed.  \n",
      "**Sources:**  \n",
      "- *Dust mite allergens: mitigation and control* (PubMed), PMID **12165210**  \n",
      "- *The Prevention of House Dust Mite Allergies in Pediatric Asthma* (PubMed), PMID **38671686**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38671686  \n",
      "- *Biomarkers for diagnosis and prediction of therapy responses in allergic diseases and asthma* (Allergy), PMID **32893900**\n",
      "\n",
      "7. **Common allergy knowledge and misconceptions**\n",
      "\n",
      "A key misconception is that **a positive allergy test automatically means clinical allergy**. The evidence says otherwise:\n",
      "\n",
      "- Positive skin tests or specific IgE can occur **without symptoms**.\n",
      "- **Sensitization ≠ clinical allergy**.\n",
      "- History, exposure timing, and symptoms must be interpreted together.\n",
      "\n",
      "Another misconception is that symptoms alone prove allergy; they do not. Rhinitis, conjunctivitis, and asthma may cluster, but diagnosis still needs clinical correlation.  \n",
      "**Sources:**  \n",
      "- *Biomarkers for diagnosis and prediction of therapy responses in allergic diseases and asthma* (Allergy), PMID **32893900**  \n",
      "- *Combinations of self-reported rhinitis, conjunctivitis, and asthma predicts IgE sensitization in more than 25,000 Danes* (Allergy), PMID **33900050**\n",
      "\n",
      "8. **Diagnosis and regular allergist consultation**\n",
      "\n",
      "Regular allergist/immunologist consultation is useful when symptoms are:\n",
      "\n",
      "- recurrent,\n",
      "- multisystem,\n",
      "- food-triggered,\n",
      "- associated with asthma,\n",
      "- suspicious for anaphylaxis,\n",
      "- or diagnostically unclear.\n",
      "\n",
      "The knowledge base also recommends bringing **exposure timing, symptom notes, medications, and photos of rashes** to improve assessment. Testing is interpreted in context; biomarker-driven precision medicine is advancing, but not all markers are ready for routine care.  \n",
      "**Source:** *Biomarkers for diagnosis and prediction of therapy responses in allergic diseases and asthma* (Allergy), PMID **32893900**\n",
      "\n",
      "9. **Current established treatments**\n",
      "\n",
      "Established care depends on the allergic syndrome, but the knowledge base supports these broad principles:\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Epinephrine** is first-line for anaphylaxis; antihistamines do **not** replace it.\n",
      "- In severe type 2 asthma, biologics targeting **IgE, IL-5/IL-5R, IL-4Rα, and TSLP** are established options in selected patients.\n",
      "- For selected plant food allergies, **component-resolved diagnostics** help risk stratify, but they are not treatment.\n",
      "- Peanut oral immunotherapy can increase desensitization in selected patients, but it is not a cure and carries adverse-event risk.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Sources:**  \n",
      "- *Standardized Grass Pollen Allergenic Extracts* (FDA)  \n",
      "- *Biomarkers for diagnosis and prediction of therapy responses in allergic diseases and asthma* (Allergy), PMID **32893900**  \n",
      "- *Precision Medicine in Plant Food Allergy: a Systematic Review of Biomarkers Under a Clinical Approach* (Clinical Reviews in Allergy & Immunology), PMID **41739332**, DOI **10.1007/s12016-026-09136-8**  \n",
      "- *Efficacy and Safety of Oral Immunotherapy for Peanut Allergy: A GRADE-Assessed Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials* (International Archives of Allergy and Immunology), PMID **40938787**, DOI **10.1159/000548282**\n",
      "\n",
      "10. **Allergen immunotherapy and immune tolerance**\n",
      "\n",
      "Allergen immunotherapy is described in the knowledge base as the **only disease-modifying treatment** for selected IgE-mediated respiratory allergies.\n",
      "\n",
      "- **SCIT** and **SLIT** are established for appropriate indications.\n",
      "- Food immunotherapy exists, but is **more limited and condition-specific**.\n",
      "- For house dust mite allergy, the evidence base supports immunotherapy as a key established approach, while availability, safety, and standardization vary.\n",
      "\n",
      "This is the clearest example of moving from symptom control toward **immune tolerance** rather than only symptom suppression.  \n",
      "**Source:** *Immunotherapy of house dust mite allergy* (PubMed), PMID **28853977**, URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28853977\n",
      "\n",
      "11. **Biologics and targeted immunology**\n",
      "\n",
      "This is where established care and recent immunology research meet.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Established clinical practice**\n",
      "- Biologics are already part of care for selected severe asthma patients, especially those with type 2 inflammation.\n",
      "- Biomarkers such as **eosinophils** and **FeNO** are more useful than total IgE alone for many treatment decisions.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Recent approved treatment**\n",
      "- **Tezepelumab**, an **anti-TSLP** biologic, is an approved example of alarmin-targeted therapy in severe asthma.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Active research**\n",
      "- Targeting **IL-33** and **IL-25** remains an active area.\n",
      "- Biomarker-driven precision medicine is expanding, but many novel biomarkers are not yet validated for routine care.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Sources:**  \n",
      "- *Biomarkers for diagnosis and prediction of therapy responses in allergic diseases and asthma* (Allergy), PMID **32893900**  \n",
      "- *Targeting alarmins in asthma: From bench to clinic* (PMC), URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12555011  \n",
      "- *The Evolution, Immunopathogenesis and Biomarkers of Type 2 Inflammation in Common Allergic Disorders* (PubMed), PMID **40586319**\n",
      "\n",
      "12. **Latest allergy/immunology research**\n",
      "\n",
      "The newest direction in allergy research is less about one test and more about a **systems view**:\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Epithelial barrier dysfunction**\n",
      "- **Microbiome alterations**\n",
      "- **Exposome effects**\n",
      "- **Alarmin biology**\n",
      "- **Biomarker-guided precision medicine**\n",
      "\n",
      "The knowledge base specifically frames these as important emerging concepts, with better support for classical biomarkers like specific IgE, eosinophils, and FeNO, and more preliminary evidence for many new epithelial or microbiome markers.  \n",
      "**Sources:**  \n",
      "- *Epithelial Barrier Theory: The Role of Exposome, Microbiome, and Barrier Function in Allergic Diseases* (PubMed), PMID **37957791**  \n",
      "- *Biomarkers for diagnosis and prediction of therapy responses in allergic diseases and asthma* (Allergy), PMID **32893900**  \n",
      "- *Targeting alarmins in asthma: From bench to clinic* (PMC)\n",
      "\n",
      "13. **What is promising but still investigational**\n",
      "\n",
      "The knowledge base separates established care from investigational ideas:\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Promising but not yet routine:** many novel epithelial, microbiome, and barrier biomarkers\n",
      "- **Active research:** IL-33 and IL-25 pathway targeting\n",
      "- **More established:** specific IgE, eosinophils, FeNO, selected component-resolved diagnostics\n",
      "- **Condition-specific but evolving:** food immunotherapy\n",
      "- **More established in selected settings:** biologics for severe asthma, especially with type 2 inflammation\n",
      "\n",
      "For peanut allergy, oral immunotherapy is supported by trial evidence for **desensitization**, but the knowledge base notes that **sustained unresponsiveness is less certain**, and adverse effects matter.  \n",
      "**Source:** *Efficacy and Safety of Oral Immunotherapy for Peanut Allergy: A GRADE-Assessed Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials* (PubMed), PMID **40938787**\n",
      "\n",
      "14. **Practical questions to discuss with an allergist**\n",
      "\n",
      "Based on the evidence retrieved, useful discussion points include:\n",
      "\n",
      "- What symptoms and exposures suggest **true clinical allergy** versus sensitization?\n",
      "- Do my symptoms fit **rhinitis/conjunctivitis**, **food allergy**, **asthma**, or **anaphylaxis risk**?\n",
      "- Which tests are most useful in my case: **specific IgE**, **component testing**, or other biomarkers?\n",
      "- What environmental measures are most realistic for my home or season?\n",
      "- Do I have an indication for **allergen immunotherapy**?\n",
      "- If severe asthma is present, are **biologic therapies** relevant?\n",
      "- Should I keep a symptom/exposure log before follow-up?\n",
      "\n",
      "**Source basis:** *Biomarkers for diagnosis and prediction of therapy responses in allergic diseases and asthma* (Allergy), PMID **32893900**\n",
      "\n",
      "15. **Bottom line**\n",
      "\n",
      "Allergy is a story that starts with **sensitization**, becomes visible through **early symptoms**, and may escalate to **emergency anaphylaxis**. Mechanistically, it is driven by **IgE and type 2 immune pathways**, with the environment shaping exposure, barrier function, and risk. Established care still relies on **history plus appropriate testing**, **epinephrine for anaphylaxis**, standard symptom control, and for selected patients **allergen immunotherapy** or **biologics**. The newest research is pushing toward **precision medicine**, but many biomarkers and epithelial/microbiome hypotheses remain **promising rather than routine**.\n",
      "\n",
      "If you want, I can turn this into a shorter one-page patient handout using the same Qdrant evidence.\n"
