      "Here are the main recent developments in allergy/immunology treatment, grouped by what is already established versus still investigational.\n",
      "\n",
      "## Established clinical options\n",
      "\n",
      "### 1) Biologics for type 2 inflammatory disease\n",
      "These are now standard options in selected patients, especially for **severe asthma** and some related allergic/type 2 conditions.\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Omalizumab (anti-IgE)**  \n",
      "  - Established for allergic asthma; also now has an FDA-approved role in **food allergy risk reduction** in selected patients.\n",
      "- **Dupilumab (anti–IL-4Rα)**  \n",
      "  - Established for severe asthma, atopic dermatitis, chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps, and eosinophilic esophagitis.\n",
      "- **Mepolizumab / Benralizumab / Reslizumab (anti–IL-5/IL-5R)**  \n",
      "  - Established options for severe eosinophilic asthma.\n",
      "- **Tezepelumab (anti-TSLP)**  \n",
      "  - Established option for severe asthma, including broader phenotype coverage than older biologics.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Evidence note:** response is often better predicted by biomarkers/phenotype than by total IgE alone. Biomarkers with routine utility include **specific IgE, blood eosinophils, and FeNO**; more novel biomarkers are still not fully validated.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 2) Allergen immunotherapy for respiratory allergy\n",
      "- **SCIT (subcutaneous immunotherapy)** and **SLIT (sublingual immunotherapy)** are established disease-modifying treatments for selected **IgE-mediated respiratory allergies** such as allergic rhinitis and some allergic asthma cases.\n",
      "- This remains the clearest “disease-modifying” treatment class in allergy.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Limitations:** product availability, allergen standardization, and safety monitoring vary by region and product.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 3) Food allergy immunotherapy in selected settings\n",
      "- **Peanut oral immunotherapy (OIT)** is now an established option in selected patients, mainly to increase desensitization.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Important limitation:** it is **not a cure**; benefits are offset by higher rates of adverse effects, including gastrointestinal symptoms and epinephrine use.\n",
      "\n",
      "## Still investigational or early-stage\n",
      "\n",
      "### 1) Food allergy biologic strategies beyond approved indications\n",
      "Research is active on:\n",
      "- biologics combined with food OIT,\n",
      "- biologic monotherapy for food allergy,\n",
      "- multi-allergen approaches.\n",
      "\n",
      "These are promising but **not broadly established** outside approved indications and specialist protocols.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 2) Novel biomarkers and precision medicine tools\n",
      "- Epithelial-barrier markers\n",
      "- Microbiome-based approaches\n",
      "- “Omics” signatures\n",
      "- New composite prediction scores\n",
      "\n",
      "These are scientifically important, but many remain **unvalidated for routine clinical decision-making**.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 3) Epithelial barrier / microbiome-targeted therapies\n",
      "The **epithelial barrier hypothesis** is a useful research framework, but therapies aimed directly at barrier or microbiome remodeling for allergic disease are mostly **early-stage**.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 4) New biologic targets\n",
      "Beyond IgE, IL-4/13, IL-5, and TSLP, other pathways are being explored, but many are still in trials or preclinical development.\n",
      "\n",
      "## Big picture: what changed most recently?\n",
      "The most important recent shift is that allergy/immunology has moved from broad symptom control to **phenotype-directed, mechanism-based treatment**, especially with:\n",
      "- more biologics for type 2 disease,\n",
      "- a stronger role for biomarker-guided selection,\n",
      "- expansion of immunotherapy approaches,\n",
      "- the first FDA-approved biologic use in food allergy risk reduction.\n",
      "\n",
      "## Evidence cautions\n",
      "- **Approved / guideline-supported** does not mean universally appropriate; these treatments are for selected patients and indications.\n",
      "- **Investigational** does not mean ineffective; it means the evidence is not yet strong enough for routine use.\n",
      "- Outcomes such as “desensitization” in food allergy are not the same as **durable remission**.\n",
      "\n",
